<h2>Preparing a Strong Appraisal Position</h2>
<p>Appraisal is decided on evidence. The side with the clearer, better-supported estimate usually has the stronger hand. Before or during appraisal, it helps to have:</p>
<ul>
<li>A complete, itemized Xactimate estimate with measurements and photos for every line</li>
<li>Pre-loss and post-loss photos and video</li>
<li>Weather data for the date of loss (see our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a>)</li>
<li>Local contractor bids reflecting real post-storm pricing</li>
<li>Code references and permit requirements where relevant</li>
<li>A clear list of the specific items in dispute and the dollar difference on each</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> service is often the first step, because it shows exactly where the two estimates diverge.</p>

<h2>Experience on Both Sides of the Table</h2>
<p>Joseph Dittman, the licensed public adjuster behind Port Arthur Public Adjuster, is also a certified insurance appraiser, insurance umpire and expert witness. His background includes carrier-side adjusting, public adjusting, appraisal work, expert witness support and hands-on construction experience across residential, commercial, storm, wind, hail, water, fire and hurricane losses. That mix is useful in appraisal, where the work comes down to explaining construction scope and pricing in a way that an opposing appraiser and an umpire can follow. You can read more on the <a href="/author/joseph-dittman/">author page</a>.</p>
<p>One practical note: many policies require each appraiser to be competent and disinterested or impartial. Whether a particular person can serve as your appraiser on a given claim depends on that language and on their role in the claim. We will walk through how your policy’s wording applies before any demand is made.</p>

<h2>What Happens After the Award</h2>
<p>An appraisal award sets the amount of loss, but it is not the same thing as the check. The insurer will typically apply the deductible, subtract prior payments and, under replacement cost policies, may hold back depreciation until repairs are completed. If the award is broken out into replacement cost and actual cash value, that breakdown affects what is paid now and what is recoverable later. Coverage defenses the insurer raised before appraisal may also still be in play. Reading the award carefully, and comparing it with your policy and payment history, helps you understand exactly what to expect and what steps, such as submitting final invoices for depreciation, remain.</p>

<h2>Costs, Timing and Risks</h2>
<p>Under many policies, each side pays its own appraiser and splits the umpire’s fees. Appraisal can resolve a dispute in weeks or take months, depending on the complexity of the loss and how quickly the panel is formed. An award may come in higher, lower or close to either side’s estimate; there is no guaranteed result. Because appraisal can affect other options, talk with a Texas attorney if the dispute also involves coverage, a deadline to sue, or allegations of misrepresentation.</p>
<p>As a Port Arthur Public Adjuster, we can help you decide whether appraisal makes sense, prepare the supporting estimate and documentation, and guide you through the process. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> to talk it through.</p>
